      "id": 1,
      "text": "Madison Heights incorporated on January 17, 1955, becoming the tenth city in Oakland County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madison_Heights%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city’s name honors President James Madison, while “Heights” hints at its modest elevation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.wisdomlib.org/cities/madison-heights-18101",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Before cityhood, the area was farmland in Royal Oak Township and later supported automotive railroads.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Madison_Heights,_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Post‑WWII, Detroit’s auto boom drew workers to Madison Heights, a suburb on the I‑75 and I‑696 corridor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Madison_Heights,_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The first city hall opened at 26305 John R Road before moving to a new building on West Thirteen Mile Road in 1963.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madison_Heights%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Madison Heights borders Troy, Royal Oak, Hazel Park, and Warren, with its eastern edge marking the Oakland‑Macomb county line.",
